WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.650
This is the brief on how screens affect
your brain and your sleep.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:10.015
Basically, think of your brain like a mix
between a calculator and a rechargeable
battery.

00:00:10.015 --> 00:00:13.949
Screens trick that calculator into being
super lazy during the day.

00:00:13.949 --> 00:00:17.526
Then they completely wreck the battery's
recharge cycle at night.

00:00:17.526 --> 00:00:20.745
First, let's look at why the calculator
gets lazy.

00:00:20.745 --> 00:00:23.606
It all comes down to your neuroeconomic
scale.

00:00:23.606 --> 00:00:26.825
Your brain is constantly weighing effort
against reward, right?

00:00:26.825 --> 00:00:30.044
Swiping gives us instant rewards for
literally zero effort.

00:00:30.044 --> 00:00:32.905
It's kind of like eating candy all day.

00:00:32.905 --> 00:00:37.197
Suddenly, eating broccoli, like doing your
homework, feels like a terrible deal.

00:00:37.197 --> 00:00:40.059
That's because your brain expects an easy
payout.

00:00:40.059 --> 00:00:42.920
Second, this daytime addiction bleeds into
the night.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.066
It stops your battery from recharging.

00:00:45.066 --> 00:00:47.212
Blue light from screens suppresses
melatonin.

00:00:47.212 --> 00:00:49.716
Melatonin is basically your brain's sleep
juice.

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:52.935
That tricks your body into thinking it's
still daytime.

00:00:52.935 --> 00:00:57.942
An MIT study proved consistent sleep
accounts for nearly 25% of a student's
grades.

00:00:57.942 --> 00:01:01.161
Sleep is when your hippocampus locks in
those memories.

00:01:01.161 --> 00:01:04.380
By scrolling at night, you aren't just
losing sleep.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:07.242
You're literally deleting what you learned
that day.

00:01:07.242 --> 00:01:10.461
Finally, you can fix this with a shutdown
ritual.

00:01:10.461 --> 00:01:14.753
About 30 to 60 minutes before bed, power
down all your screens.

00:01:14.753 --> 00:01:19.402
Tidy your space, read a physical fiction
book, or do some light stretching.

00:01:19.402 --> 00:01:21.906
That lets your brain safely cool down.

00:01:21.906 --> 00:01:26.913
If you're thinking, but my phone is my
alarm clock, buy a regular clock.

00:01:26.913 --> 00:01:32.278
Or put your phone across the room so you
physically can't reach it to scroll.

00:01:32.278 --> 00:01:36.213
Ultimately, give your brain a break from
the screen at night.

00:01:36.213 --> 00:01:40.147
It'll reward you with sharper focus and
way better grades tomorrow.
